From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: a new compute program's samplers and shadow uniforms

A program's sampler units and shadow-comparison uniforms (shadmask/shadadd) are uniforms of that program, which vrend
sets only for sampler views marked dirty. A draw that switches programs marks every view dirty; a dispatch that
switches compute programs didn't, so a compute program used after another one with the same views bound read all its
textures through unit 0 and every shadow lookup as 0 (the shadow uniforms' default): GravityMark's Direct3D 11
lighting (a compute pass) had the whole scene in shadow - no sunlight, the exposure brightening the ambient light
into a flat, flickering image. A dispatch's new program now marks its views and constant buffers dirty, as a draw's.
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c	2026-10-26 16:47:13.910229095 +0100
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c	2026-10-26 17:17:03.664263776 +0100
@@ -6348,6 +6348,11 @@
          sub_ctx->prog_ids[PIPE_SHADER_COMPUTE] = sub_ctx->shaders[PIPE_SHADER_COMPUTE]->current->id;
          sub_ctx->prog = prog;
          prog->ref_context = sub_ctx;
+         /* as a draw's new program: its sampler units and shadow uniforms are its own, set only for dirty views
+          * (without this a compute program read its textures through unit 0 and its shadow lookups as 0 - in
+          * shadow - when the views stayed bound: GravityMark's Direct3D 11 lighting lost the sun) */
+         sub_ctx->const_bufs_dirty[PIPE_SHADER_COMPUTE] = ~0;
+         sub_ctx->views[PIPE_SHADER_COMPUTE].dirty_mask = ~0;
       }
       sub_ctx->shader_dirty = true;
       sub_ctx->cs_shader_dirty = false;
